    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-spec-lifecycle"}}

    {{> "axiom/truth/ax-spec-is-sole-source"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-dx-first"}}

    <Axiom id="AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM">
      The diagram floor for a scope spec is a fixed ladder, not one "when useful" picture — the
      same discipline agent-inbox's `AX_COMPREHENSION_LADDER` applies to a review applies here at
      design time instead of review time. Four rungs, each answering ONE distinct question, drawn
      in THIS order — requirements close BEFORE Use Cases and Data Flow are drawn, never the
      reverse, per the 2026-08-20 visualization-chain decision
      (`specs/ai-skills/research/2026-08-20-visualization-chain.research.md`): drawing the
      scenario picture only once the failure-mode requirements are locked means a failure branch
      on the diagram already traces to a real requirement ID, not a guess.
      1. **Overview** — WHAT this scope is, system-context / high-level view. Unconditional. Held
         by tooling: `sdd-check` fails a missing section (`SDD_NO_DIAGRAM_BLOCK`) or an empty one
         (`SDD_DIAGRAM_BLOCK_EMPTY`).
      2. **High-Level Architecture** — WHERE this scope sits AMONG its neighbours (C4-style
         boundary: this scope as a box, confirmed dependencies/consumers as boxes outside it) —
         distinct from Overview (Overview = the shape at a glance; this = the boundary, decided
         once Architecture is locked). Unconditional, drawn at `STEP_5_ARCHITECTURE` — see that
         step's Action for the concrete rule.
      3. **Use Cases** — `product` scope-type only: actors → scenario, one line «зачем» each, the
         worst-case scenario marked distinctly (⚠️) — harvested from the «Потребители» / «Худший
         сценарий» topics closed during the `STEP_2_INTENT_CAPTURE` interview, but drawn only once
         `STEP_3_REQUIREMENTS_GATE` closes — see that step's Action for the concrete rule.
      4. **Data Flow** — `product` AND `library`: a `flowchart` built from exactly four node kinds
         (external entity, process, store, labelled data-flow arrow) — NO conditions, NO loops,
         this is a data diagram, not a control-flow chart. Unconditional for both scope-types,
         drawn at `STEP_3_REQUIREMENTS_GATE` right after Use Cases — see that step's Action.
      All four are drawn as ASCII in chat at the step that owns them, BEFORE that step's Approval
      Check — a diagram appearing for the first time in the written spec means the operator never
      saw the shape they are approving (`AX_CHAT_BEFORE_SPEC`, `formats/diagram-vocabulary.xml`);
      an Approval Check without it is a show-before-ask violation the operator may reject and
      demand, the same class as the router's `H_ASK_WITHOUT_CARD`. Every diagram carries a caption
      naming the requirement IDs it shows, per `DIAGRAM_CAPTION_FORMAT`
      (`formats/diagram-vocabulary.xml`) — Overview and High-Level Architecture, being
      whole-scope pictures, may use the no-ID caption form the same contract allows. This ladder
      (rungs 1–4) is the mandatory floor for NEW scope specs only — a scope spec written before
      rungs 3–4 existed is not retroactively broken by their absence. Beyond this floor the author
      adds more views (sequence, before/after) wherever a further diagram genuinely helps a
      reader; that stays judgment, never a gate.
    </Axiom>

