  <SessionState>
    {{> "contract/process/session-file-format"}}
  </SessionState>

  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NO_INTAKE` | Empty intake |
    | `H_AMBIGUOUS_MODE` | Spec exists + mode verb absent (per `AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT`) |
    | `H_AMBIGUOUS_PRODUCT_LIBRARY` | Operator intent leaves product vs library unclear after STEP_1_CONFIRM |
    | `H_LOW_CONFIDENCE` | Confidence `LOW` while locking architecture |
    | `H_REQ_NOT_APPROVED` | Design Variants attempted without a closed Requirements Gate |
    | `H_GOLDEN_NOT_APPROVED` | Design Variants attempted without an approved Usage Example |
    | `H_SYSTEM_FIT_NOT_RUN` | `STEP_5_ARCHITECTURE` entered before `STEP_4B_SYSTEM_FIT`'s checklist closed, or "вводим новое" recorded without the specific neighbour invariant/requirement ID it fails (`AX_PARALLEL_MECHANISM_REQUIRES_JUSTIFICATION`) |
    | `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE` | A rule reference does not resolve to an existing file |
    | `H_PIVOT_NO_INVALIDATION_LIST` | `pivot` mode without a Pivot Invalidation List |
    | `H_REWRITE_WITH_DOWNSTREAM` | `rewrite` mode but the scope already has module specs / tickets — not v0, use `pivot` (`AX_PIVOT_REQUIRES_SUPERSESSION`) |
    | `H_BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS_MISSING` | STEP_8 finished without a Bootstrap Requirements section |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECT` | Operator rejected the spec |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_INTAKE">
      <Goal>Resolve scope name and mode; gather context.</Goal>
      <Action>
        State from the router already gives the portal + scope-type (product / library). Resolve the
        scope name from intake (or ask). Stat `specs/<scope>/<scope>.spec.md`: absent → `greenfield`;
        present → detect the mode per `AX_DIRECTIVE_MODE` / `AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT`. Read the
        rule registry for available rules (per `AX_SCOPE_RULES_DECLARATION`).
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_CONFIRM">
      <Goal>Confirm product vs library and outline the section set.</Goal>
      <Action>Carry the router's scope-type and mode forward. Ask only if product vs library remains genuinely ambiguous; otherwise outline the sections inline with STEP_2 and spend no dedicated Ask.</Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_INTENT_CAPTURE">
      <Goal>Lock the scope's goal and primary constraints.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Intent Candidate (1–3 bullets). Evidence Hygiene per `AX_EVIDENCE_HYGIENE` (Facts /
        Assumptions / Hypotheses, separated). Then run the operator interview —
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/interview-protocol.directive.xml")` — passing
        the scope-type (product / library — selects the TopicCatalog block) and the confirmed
        `scale:` from the session file: per `AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH` the map depth follows
        scale — `fix` / `function` → base topics + the triage minimum; `product` → the full
        scope-type block. The interview IS the substance of the step. A dispute or side-topic
        raised mid-interview opens a nested frame on the flow's stack — this step's main path
        never silently stalls on it (`AX_STACK_BASED_FLOW`). Capture the initial Runtime
        Backing posture per `AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_EXPLICIT`.

        For `product` scope-type, this step closes the «Потребители» and «Худший сценарий» topics
        — the raw material for the Use Cases diagram. Per the 2026-08-20 visualization-chain
        decision (requirements close BEFORE use cases are drawn, never the reverse), this step
        does NOT draw that diagram — the drawing happens at `STEP_3_REQUIREMENTS_GATE`, once the
        Requirements Gate closes (rung 3 of `AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`); see that step's Action.
        Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_REQUIREMENTS_GATE">
      <Goal>Close the requirements.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Functional · Non-Functional · Out-of-Scope. For product: + Runtime Backing (per
        `AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_EXPLICIT`) + Project-Wide Rules (per `AX_SCOPE_RULES_DECLARATION`).
        Close the remaining holes via
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/interview-protocol.directive.xml")` — focus:
        only the topics the Requirements Gate still misses; map depth sized to the session-file
        `scale:` per `AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH`.

        Once the requirements list is drafted — including the нештатная entries, a failure branch
        without a locked requirement ID has nothing to caption — draw the two diagrams the
        2026-08-20 visualization-chain decision moves to this point, per
        `AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`:
        1. `product` scope-type ONLY — the Use Cases diagram (rung 3): actors → scenario nodes,
           one line «зачем» each, harvested from the «Потребители» / «Худший сценарий» topics
           closed at `STEP_2_INTENT_CAPTURE`, the worst-case scenario marked distinctly (⚠️) — as
           its own ASCII block in chat; a bare bullet list of user types does not satisfy this
           rung.
        2. `product` AND `library` — the Data Flow diagram (rung 4, `DFD` type per
           `formats/diagram-vocabulary.xml`): a `flowchart` built from exactly four node kinds
           — external entity, process, store, labelled data-flow arrow — NO conditions, NO loops
           (a data diagram, not a control-flow chart); ≤7 nodes; as its own ASCII block in chat.
        Each diagram's caption names the requirement IDs it shows, per `DIAGRAM_CAPTION_FORMAT`
        (`formats/diagram-vocabulary.xml`) — `_<что показывает> — <ACR>-REQ-<N>[,
        <ACR>-REQ-<M>]._`. **Both diagrams shown (Data Flow always; Use Cases when
        scope-type=product), this message, before the Approval Check below** — their absence is
        a show-before-ask violation, the same class as the router's `H_ASK_WITHOUT_CARD`; the
        operator may reject and demand them. Record the closed set for STEP_5's consolidated design
        packet; ask here only if a blocking requirement fork remains (`H_REQ_NOT_APPROVED`).
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_GOLDEN_DX">
      <Goal>Lock the Approved Usage Example (mandatory before architecture).</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per `AX_DX_FIRST`, write a real-code usage example: library → Public API DX; product →
        UX-flow or CLI DX (depth per Project Type). Carry it into STEP_5's consolidated design
        packet; ask here only if a new fork remains (else `H_GOLDEN_NOT_APPROVED`).
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4B_SYSTEM_FIT">
      <Goal>Reconcile with the existing system before architecture locks.</Goal>
      <Action>
        FIRST action this step: run orientation ToolCall below (this works even before
        `sdd-new` materializes the spec — a
        greenfield scope-type is not a reason to skip the call). Read its output in full: the portal
        line, the one-hop neighbours with their paths, entity/contract NAMES, requirement IDs + titles,
        the consumers list, and its closing question — the tool's bounded extract is the evidence, not
        a repo scan (`AX_BOOTSTRAP_NARROW_READ` governs the LATER code-level narrow read at STEP_8; this
        read is spec-level, via the tool, and comes first).

        Then close, in THIS message, the fixed checklist — record every answer in the spec (Decision
        Log entry when (a)/(b) lands on "new", inline note otherwise):
        (a) Is this entity/mechanism already an existing kind — does the extract already name it?
        (b) If not: which existing mechanism the extract names are we EXTENDING instead of introducing
        a new one?
        (c) Which neighbour specs (paths, from the extract) will need work for this to fit the system —
        each becomes its own «СОСЕДИ» line in the eventual `CHANGE_MANIFEST` / Pivot Invalidation List
        sideways entry?
        (d) Which neighbour requirements (IDs, from the extract) conflict with what we are about to
        lock, and how is the conflict resolved?
        "Вводим новое" answers (a)/(b) ONLY together with the specific neighbour invariant/requirement
        ID the existing mechanism cannot satisfy, plus its own Decision Log entry
        (`AX_PARALLEL_MECHANISM_REQUIRES_JUSTIFICATION`) — "проще с нуля" is not a citation, else
        `H_SYSTEM_FIT_NOT_RUN`. On a genuinely greenfield project with no neighbours, `sdd-orient`
        degenerates to its one-line "соседей нет" — the checklist still runs, (a) still gets an honest
        answer, (c)/(d) collapse to "н/д"; the step itself is never skipped. These answers feed
        STEP_5's consolidated design packet; they are not a dedicated confirmation surface.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="orientation">npx gennady sdd-orient --scope <scope></ToolCall> Inputs <scope>: <scope>=operator-confirmed scope retained by this flow. Use orientation.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_5_ARCHITECTURE">
      <Goal>Lock the architecture.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Research gate before drafting: at scale `product` / `library` — mandatory, 2–3 targeted
        WebSearch queries on how market leaders solve THIS scope's requirements and how they
        architect for them; at scale `module` / `function` — proportional per
        `AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH` (light pass, or skip with a one-line rationale); at scale
        `fix` — skipped, no research. Where it runs, persist it per `AX_RESEARCH_PERSISTED` before
        the Design Variants / Architecture below are drafted with researchManifest ToolCall below, filled
        (STATUS/PROBLEM/CRITERIA/OPTIONS/DECISION/CONSEQUENCES/EVIDENCE/RELATED) — and link the
        document in this message and in the Decision Log entry's RELATED (parentheses) once the
        variant is picked at STEP_6.
        Inputs <scope>, <slug>: this STEP_5 research context.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="researchManifest">npx gennady sdd-new research --scope <scope> --slug <slug></ToolCall> Use researchManifest.

        library → Architecture: public surface structure, key patterns, briefly-rejected alternatives.
        product → Design Variants per `AX_DIVERGE_BEFORE_RECOMMEND` — reach past the obvious
        option into the tail; judge each against THIS scope, do not collapse to the modal design.
        Variant count follows the session-file `scale:` per `AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH` (Project
        Type refines it per `AX_PRODUCT_LIBRARY_FLOW`): scale `product` → 2–3 variants; scale
        `module` / `function` → 1–2, or a single design with the skip rationale recorded in the
        Decision Log. Under `LOW` confidence do not lock (`H_LOW_CONFIDENCE`).

        Before the Approval Check, draw the scope-boundary diagram (rung 2 of
        `AX_SPEC_MANDATORY_DIAGRAM`, C4-style, per `formats/diagram-vocabulary.xml`): THIS scope as
        a boundary box, its known neighbours as boxes outside it — depends-on (infra-base / other
        scopes, from the Portal Scope Graph already in state) and provides-to (consumer scopes),
        plus whatever Scope Dependencies are confirmed so far — ≤7 nodes, as an ASCII block in THIS
        message. This IS the Overview system-context shape decided now, for THIS scope's place
        among neighbours; `STEP_9_FINAL_SPEC` converts the same drawing to mermaid inside both
        `## Overview` and `## High-Level Architecture` (`AX_CHAT_BEFORE_SPEC`) — never invented
        fresh at write time. **Diagram shown, this message, before the Approval Check below** —
        its absence is a show-before-ask violation, the same class as the router's
        `H_ASK_WITHOUT_CARD`; the operator may reject and demand it. Present requirements, Usage
        Example, architecture, system-fit consequences, and non-default rule deltas as ONE
        consolidated design packet. Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_6_ARCHITECTURE_CHOICE">
      <Goal>Product only: pick a variant.</Goal>
      <Action>
        library → skip to STEP_7. product → operator picks a variant; record a Decision Log entry per
        `AX_DECISION_LOG_REQUIRED` (`DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT`). STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_7_RULE_ACTIVATION">
      <Goal>Lock the active rule set for this scope.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run the rule activation per `AX_RULE_ACTIVATION` against the registry read at STEP_0:
        1. Stage A — per category, shortlist «recommend activate» + «considered, recommend skip»,
           each with a one-line rationale scoped to THIS scope. Operator agrees / expands /
           contracts → list locked; deviations → Decision Log entry (`DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT`).
        2. Stage B — for `architecture/*` and `infra/*` rules on the locked list: read only
           `Mission`, `Belief_State`, `Reward_Criteria` of the rule file; surface each axiom with a
           rationale → operator Accept (default) / Adapt / Override — Adapt and Override each land
           in the Decision Log. Keep it light on uncontroversial axioms. `coding/*` / `testing/*`
           stay deferred to phase entry.
        Record the locked list in Project-Wide Rules per `AX_SCOPE_RULES_DECLARATION`; a reference
        that resolves to no file → `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE`. Defaults implied by the selected stack
        require no Ask; only Adapt/Override deltas reopen the consolidated design decision.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_8_EXTERNAL_DEPENDENCIES_AUDIT">
      <Goal>Reconcile the drafted spec with repository reality; materialize Bootstrap Requirements.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Walk the drafted spec; extract every external assumption (packages, workspace links, tools,
        pre-existing files, cross-scope types, env / services, structural prerequisites). Classify each
        `exists` | `missing` | `unknown` — narrow read for inventory only, per `AX_BOOTSTRAP_NARROW_READ`
        and `AX_SPEC_IS_SOLE_SOURCE` (no reading app source for design). Resolve every `missing` /
        `unknown` with the operator (source · version · owner), each a Decision Log entry. Record
        every confirmed cross-scope assumption as a `depends-on` edge in Scope Dependencies per
        `AX_CROSS_SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES` (cross-scope contracts come from `interface` scopes). Materialize
        the Bootstrap Requirements table. Readiness rows additionally name exact `Readiness Gates`
        and repo-relative `Gate Artifacts`; non-readiness rows use `—`. Gate: empty list
        only if explicitly «nothing to bootstrap», else `H_BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS_MISSING`. An existing
        mechanism this audit turns up feeds back into Architecture / Contracts now, in the SAME session
        — per `AX_BOOTSTRAP_NARROW_READ`, pop back to the relevant earlier step, incorporate it, re-run
        this audit once more; only a finding raised after the spec has already merged to master needs a
        formal `pivot`.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_9_FINAL_SPEC">
      <Goal>Emit the scope spec.</Goal>
      <Action>
        In `greenfield`/`rewrite` mode, when `specs/<scope>/<scope>.spec.md` does not exist yet, create
        its skeleton via productManifest ToolCall below (or
        libraryManifest ToolCall below) —
        the command never overwrites and prints the section manifest (which sections are REQUIRED and
        what to fill); in `refine`/`pivot` mode the file already exists, edit it in place, skip
        `sdd-new`. Content guidance per `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE` the matching format —
        `ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/product-spec-structure.xml`
        (product) or `ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/library-spec-structure.xml` (library); Bootstrap
        Requirements is part of every spec. Keep the
        spec inside its bounded role per `AX_SCOPE_STAYS_THIN` — entity / contract / file-level detail
        belongs to module specs. Before writing, run the self-check pass per
        `AX_ARTIFACT_STYLE_SELF_CHECK`. In `refine` mode update sections in place per `AX_LIVING_SPEC`. In `pivot`
        mode also `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/pivot-formats.xml")` (Pivot
        Invalidation List + rework entries) per `AX_PIVOT_REQUIRES_SUPERSESSION`; in `rewrite` mode write
        fresh — no supersession, no invalidation list. For a brand-new scope, append the
        `🚧 (awaiting root sync)` placeholder row to the portal Scopes table per
        `AX_PORTAL_READ_ONLY_WITH_PLACEHOLDER_EXCEPTION` — every other portal edit belongs to the
        root flow. Write in review-state per `AX_SPEC_LIFECYCLE` —
        prepend a `CHANGE_MANIFEST` (`READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/change-manifest.xml")`)
        and mark changed lines `✚`/`~` (greenfield → manifest only, no per-line marks) — its «СОСЕДИ»
        block carries the (c)/(d) answers `STEP_4B_SYSTEM_FIT` recorded, one line per neighbour path,
        never left empty by omission; compress folds it to
        master downstream after the change ships. Before internal critic,
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/preflight-protocol.directive.xml")` and run
        its lazy active-domain checklist. Then close with the integrity check per
        `AX_CLOSE_WITH_INTEGRITY_CHECK`: run integrityCheck ToolCall below (anchor
        balance + required sections + links) and resolve any finding. Then
        Branch by decomposition and impact — missing/ambiguous evidence fails closed:
        - New / rewritten `product` or `library`, or `refine` / `pivot` with incomplete decomposition:
          do NOT
          run critic or external review yet. Keep the scope draft in review-state. Only after this
          step's final Approval Check is accepted, run sessionWorkset ToolCall below,
          then re-enter the `/sdd` router with intent `module-decomposition` per
          `AX_HANDOFF_TO_MODULE_DECOMPOSITION`; its real module directive creates/resolves the modules
          and reviews one integrated target-set only after boundaries exist. Completeness is structural:
          the non-empty Module Map must resolve one-to-one to every canonical module spec on disk.
        - `refine` / `pivot` changes a module boundary or contract: first use the `module` flow to
          update every affected module in review-state. Then review one integrated target-set = changed
          scope + ALL module specs; unchanged modules are read-only members.
        - `refine` / `pivot` is scope-only and leaves all module boundaries/contracts unchanged, with
          decomposition already complete: keep
          existing modules as read-only critic members. Do NOT add `CHANGE_MANIFEST`, line marks, or
          review-state to them. Review one target-set whose sole write-set member is the scope, while
          the review-set still names every module spec.
        A standalone `infrastructure` scope is the sole no-module exception, but its actually changed
        scope artifact still runs the same review lifecycle. Load
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/review-lifecycle.directive.xml")` once for the
        selected target-set. Only the integrated product/library bundle (or reviewed infrastructure
        exception) may reach master. After a scope-only refine/pivot returns from integrated review,
        determine its downstream outcome once: no task/DAG impact → finish as a spec-only change and
        enter the terminal-candidate handling below; task/DAG impact → load scaffold and extend the existing DAG;
        return `TerminalDecision: continue` in this same session.
        The new/incomplete-decomposition handoff above and every module-boundary handoff also return
        `continue`; neither closes the session. No task/DAG impact → spec-only terminal candidate. If
        any chained work remains, offer only continue or «Пауза — продолжу позже»; pause returns
        `TerminalDecision: pause` and preserves the scratch. Only after proving
        `unfinished chained work: none` may the terminal menu offer literal «Complete / finish»;
        after that explicit selection return `TerminalDecision: complete` and run exact
        terminal ToolCall below once. Its decision source is this STEP_9 terminal answer, not
        authoring approval, critic CLEAN, external merge, or no-impact classification. Never silently stop between review
        and this disposition, and do not create a new Ask unless impact or the terminal
        choice is genuinely unresolved. The final Approval Check itself returns `pause` while awaiting
        its answer and never closes the session. STOP.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="productManifest">npx gennady sdd-new product --scope <scope></ToolCall> Use productManifest.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="libraryManifest">npx gennady sdd-new library --scope <scope></ToolCall> Use libraryManifest.
        <ToolCall owner="this-step" result="integrityCheck">npx gennady sdd-check --all specs/<scope></ToolCall> Use integrityCheck.
        Inputs <scope-spec-path>: this STEP_9 authored scope path. Write the literal
